# Techjays deck

Turn content the user points you at (a proposal, brief, report, notes, a URL, an
existing doc) into a clean, executive-ready **.pptx** in the Techjays house style.

The look is editorial and confident: white content pages, oversized bold headlines,
uppercase letter-spaced micro-labels, generous whitespace, one dominant brand colour plus two
supporting accents used only on small marks, a faint corner watermark, a confidential
footer on every page, and **dark slides used as section separators**. The structural
idea is a tight **core deck** (executive level, ~6 slides) followed by an **appendix**
that holds all the detail — so an exec can read the core and skip the rest.

## What's in this skill
- `references/design-system.md` — the complete visual spec (colour/type tokens, exact
  coordinates, every slide pattern). **Read it before building.**
- `scripts/build_deck.js` — a working pptxgenjs generator that produces the full
  reference deck. This is your starting point: copy it, edit CONFIG + the CONTENT
  blocks, run it.
- `assets/` — Techjays brand marks: `logo.png` (dark wordmark, white pages),
  `logo_white.png` (white wordmark, dark pages), `bird.png` and `bird_white.png`
  (the small watermark/footer mark).

## Workflow

### 1. Get the content first (don't touch layout yet)
Read whatever the user pointed at — the doc, PDF, notes, URL, or prior deck. If it's a
PDF/doc, extract the substance; if a URL, fetch it. Pull out: the headline offer, the
2–4 key numbers (price, timeline, ROI/hours, counts), the main sections/modules, the
timeline, and — separately — the fine-grained detail (feature lists, per-item outcomes,
task-level schedule). If the source is thin, ask the user for the missing pieces
(especially the numbers and the deadline), or state your assumptions and proceed.

### 2. Decide the core / appendix split — this is the core idea
Core slides say *what it is and why it matters*; the appendix holds *the proof and the
detail*. Keep core slides to a single, uncluttered idea each. A typical core is:
cover → executive summary (+ KPI row) → solution summary → timeline (high level) →
investment/cost → closing. Everything granular — full feature lists, "what the client
gets" point-by-point, the week-by-week schedule — moves to the appendix. When a core
slide starts filling with bullets, that's the signal to move them to an appendix detail
slide and leave a one-line summary behind.

This isn't only for proposals. For a **report**: core = findings/recommendations,
appendix = methodology + data tables. For an **update**: core = status + decisions,
appendix = per-workstream detail. The generator's slide types map cleanly onto these.

### 3. Read the design system, then adapt the generator
Read `references/design-system.md`. Then copy `scripts/build_deck.js` and the four
`assets/*.png` into your working directory, and edit:
- **CONFIG** — vendor, client, date, output filename, and (if co-branding) a client
  logo path. If the brand isn't Techjays, swap `C1` to the brand's colour and pick two
  restrained supporting tones for `C2`/`C3`.
- **The CONTENT blocks** (marked `► CONTENT`) — the words. Each slide block is
  self-contained and commented; keep the layout code, change the strings and the data
  arrays (`kpis`, `mods`, `phases`, `detail`, `gantt`). Add or drop slides by copying a
  block. Update the page markers (`02 / 06`, `APPENDIX · A1 / A3`) to match your count.
- Keep the **watermark + footer** (they're automatic via `slide()`/`darkSlide()`), keep
  **dark slides only for separators** (appendix divider, thank-you), and keep timelines
  as **Gantt** in the appendix.

Writing voice: headlines are short, concrete, and decisive — no wordplay, no clever
subtext that depends on other slides. Bold only the phrase that carries the point.
Colour is meaning, not decoration: use `C2`/`C3` to distinguish items (module 01 vs 02,
timeline steps, Gantt workstreams), never to prettify body text.

### 4. Build, validate, QA, deliver
Build and check with the **pptx skill's** tooling (that skill covers pptxgenjs gotchas,
validation, and rendering — lean on it):
```
node build_deck.js
python <pptx-skill>/scripts/office/validate.py <out>.pptx        # must pass
python <pptx-skill>/scripts/office/soffice.py --headless --convert-to pdf <out>.pptx
pdftoppm -jpeg -r 130 <out>.pdf slide                            # then view every slide
```
Look at every rendered slide for overflow, collisions, and off-canvas shapes (the most
common failures). Fix in the generator, not the packed XML, and re-render. Then deliver
the `.pptx` to the user (and, in Cowork, write it back to their folder if they want it
there). Font note: the deck uses a single typeface (`CFG.font`, default **Google Sans**).
Google Sans is proprietary — it renders only where installed and isn't in the Google
Slides font picker, so confirm with the user where the deck will be shown. If it needs to
look identical everywhere, set `CFG.font` to **Poppins** (close, freely available) or
**Arial** (universal). See design-system.md §3.

## Client logos
This environment can't download images from the web. Get a client logo from a file the
user provides or from an existing asset (a prior PDF/deck) — `pdfimages` to extract,
crop tight, drop the background to transparent. Watch the colour: a white-on-transparent
mark vanishes on white pages, so crop a dark/brand-coloured version for the cover, or
place it on a small white chip when it must sit on a dark slide.

## Quick reference — the eleven reference slides
1 cover (co-brand logos) · 2 exec summary + KPI row · 3 solution summary · 4 timeline
(high level) · 5 investment · 6 closing · **7 appendix divider (dark)** · 8–9 module
detail (what we build / what the client gets) · 10 Gantt · **11 thank-you (dark)**.
Bend this to the content — it's a vocabulary of slide types, not a fixed running order.
